  "question": {
    "text": "A body of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>200</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math>&nbsp;is tied to a spring of spring constant&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>,&nbsp;while the other end of spring is fixed at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi></math>. If the body moves about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi></math> in a circular path on a smooth horizontal surface with constant angular speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>rad</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, then the ratio of extension in the spring to its natural length will be :",
    "image": null
  },
